            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kate"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good recipe. The only suggestion that I have is that the vinegar is very strong when you first mix it. If you can make it the day before and let it mellow, use all of the vinegar, but if you need to use it right away, I would cut down on the amount of vinegar."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Nancy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Delicious. I subbed 1/8 tsp. ground ginger and used the sauce with one pound of whole wheat linguine, and sauteed shallots, snap peas, mushrooms, and grated carrots. Great flavor. Next time, I would double the sauce.",
            "datePublished": "2011-06-04T13:46:39.297Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mary Becker Weathers"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is fabulous and I hate peanut butter. I do like it in sauces, however, and this recipe is just the right amount of everything. I did cook it until boiling to mix the flavors and added 2 T. water as it was too thick for me. Hubby liked it too. Will make again.",
            "datePublished": "2021-07-13T22:27:16.923Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kate Benkendorf"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It has good ingredients, waaaay too much soy sauce so I will do a TBS next time",
            "datePublished": "2023-03-28T19:34:39.631Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Boukey"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Really yummy. Like others suggested, I used warm water in place of the olive oil and natural peanut butter (no sugar), so it wasn't too sweet. I used crunchy peanut butter for a little texture and extra chilies for a bit more heat. This will definitely be my 'go to' peanut sauce recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2010-01-23T21:39:46.397Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "DJL"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I added 1 cup of crunchy peanut butter, it got a little thick then added 3Tbsp of water thinned it right up ! This is a keeper!!!!!",
            "datePublished": "2024-03-14T23:00:51.999Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Bart"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Followed the recipe minus the red pepper flakes. Too much soy sauce and not enough peanut butter flavor. I ended up adding some coconut milk which made the soy sauce less obvious and added creaminess. Also doubled the peanut butter. With these adjustments, it was OK. Most traditional Thai peanut sauce recipes include coconut milk. It certainly helped this recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2023-08-20T22:55:53.582Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Michelle Dewey"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have been experimenting with many peanut noodle recipes (trying to find the perfect one that I can remember having growing up!) \n\nI halved the rice vinegar (a bit strong) and I halved the crushed red pepper and ginger, and used about a quarter of the garlic. This is a VERY flavorful dish, perfect balance of spicy and creamy. My family enjoyed it - they do not eat much normally, but went back for thirds for this! \n\nAnyone should try this! They will love it.",
            "datePublished": "2016-09-08T11:05:10.740Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "kelty"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Love this sauce! I omit both the oils and use a little less PB to make it lower fat, but it's still delicious, easy and versatile. Good with noodles, and I often serve it over any sort of steamed veg (makes bland vegetables taste great) and brown rice with curry-seasoned chicken. Agree with the riesling pairing, would be good with any aromatic, hi-acidity white; I had it with a fruity tempranillo the other day which was good, too.",
            "datePublished": "2012-04-16T21:26:16.807Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ACR4990"
            },
            "reviewBody": "YUM! Was a bit worried when combining the ingredients at the proportion of everything but wow perfect perfect. I just added extra ginger and garlic. Delish! Sauteed shrimp in sesame oil with red pepper flakes and chopped onion, stir fried with rice noodles, some of this sauce and a little bit of water for a perfect pasta dish. Wasnt expecting much really out of this sauce but what a great zing! Doesnt just taste like boring peanut butter!!",
            "datePublished": "2011-11-09T14:52:37.323Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "debberdee"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great recipe and s big hit with the family! I added 8 oz of lite coconut milk and used it as a sauce for chicken, red pepper, baby corn &amp; broccoli stir fry. Mixed in some softened Thai noodles before serving. Could also be served over rice instead of noodles to soak up the wonderful sauce.",
            "datePublished": "2012-06-13T19:38:17.507Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Richard"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I'm used to coconut creme in the peanut sauce. If I was to make it again I'd add maybe as much as 6 oz of coconut creme depending on the size of the batch and omit the olive oil. Other ingredients might need to be adjusted so the flavor doesn't dilute with the additional liquid volume. Recipe as given is ok, just a bit salty, so either reduce the soy or go to a less salty dark soy. Using honey over sugar is a nice twist",
            "datePublished": "2026-03-09T12:27:58.877Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Alyssa Thomas"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We've been making this for years now to eat with spring rolls. It is my absolute favorite recipe for peanut sauce. Others I have tried are too runny, and I fully agree with the author - not peanuty enough! Thank you for the recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2025-12-26T04:18:40.548Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Maureen"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Delicious - a keeper for us. Sauce is thick and creamy.We used manuka honey but felt it didn’t add the needed flavor. Next time we’ll use the regular honey. We put this over noodles and had a very enjoyable dinner.",
            "datePublished": "2025-10-04T17:30:26.106Z"
          },
